Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Connect the bucket allocator's lifetime transitions to the adapter
Upstream: apr-util 1.6.3
Upstream-Archive: https://archive.apache.org/dist/apr/apr-util-1.6.3.tar.gz
Upstream-SHA256: 2b74d8932703826862ca305b094eef2983c27b39d5c9414442e9976a9acf1983
Build-Variants: native port, Capstone domain
Depends-On: 0001-freestanding-includes
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

The bucket allocator is a client of APR's allocator: it takes 8 KiB blocks,
carves small nodes from them by bumping first_avail, files a freed small node
on its own LIFO freelist by writing the link into the freed node, and returns
whole blocks when it is destroyed. Large nodes are whole APR nodes and already
pass through the pool port's hooks.

Six hunks connect the small-node path to the adapter, and every one leaves
upstream's decisions where they were: which block, which node, in which order.
  aprb_block_lend     after apr_allocator_alloc of a block: the block is lent to
                      this allocator to carve, and the alias upstream keeps for
                      the header is what the adapter returns
  aprb_carve          replaces the bump of first_avail: the same bytes, the
                      same order, under authority of their own
  aprb_file           replaces the freelist push, which wrote a link into the
                      freed node -- a region that has just been revoked cannot
                      hold it, so the list lives in the adapter's records
  aprb_reissue        replaces the freelist pop; the node's header is rewritten
                      because a revoked region gives nothing back
  aprb_blocks_returning before the block chain goes back to APR, so the records
                      of what was carved from it are dropped with it
  aprb_probe          the first thing apr_bucket_free does with the pointer it
                      is handed: a labelled read through its own authority

The list struct is still the first piece of the first block, as upstream
carves it. list->freelist stays in the struct and stays NULL.

